  "final_text": "Both objects exist, and the rule is enabled and has run successfully.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-db13e054 Web logs overview\"** (ID `2825d451-d2ef-4cae-8d74-ced1919b89b8`)\n- Header markdown panel.\n- Four metric panels: total requests, unique clients (`clientip`), average response size (`bytes`), and total bytes served.\n- \"Requests over time\" line chart on `timestamp`.\n- \"Requests by response code\" donut on `response.keyword`, top 5 values.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-db13e054 5xx spike\"** (ID `a2eda3c0-190a-47e3-8ece-640764990d70`)\n- Type `.es-query`, data view `kibana_sample_data_logs`, schedule every 1m.\n- Condition: count of documents where `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"` over a 5m window, `> 10`.\n- Enabled. Its first run on 03:16 succeeded with 0 alerts.\n\n**Choices and limits:**\n- The data is mostly the sample dataset's fields as listed in the data view. I could not run aggregations to check actual values, because Kibana's search and ES|QL routes are disabled in this environment. So I haven't confirmed the sample data contains 5xx responses, and the rule may never fire on this data.\n- The 5xx match uses a lexical range on the `response.keyword` string field, which works for three-digit status codes. The `response` field is text, so that is the only exact-match option.\n- I left out the dashboard's `access_control` setting. With it included, Kibana returned a 500 on every full-panel create. The dashboard is still created with the default access settings.\n- While working out that 500, I created and then deleted about a dozen test dashboards titled `BISECT-\u2026`. The final list query returns no leftovers from this session.",
